<section class="section drawing-guide" id="read-a-septic-drawing" aria-labelledby="drawing-guide-heading">
    <div class="container">
        <header class="drawing-guide__header">
            <span class="eyebrow">Inside a returned agency file</span>
            <h2 id="drawing-guide-heading">What can you actually read from a septic drawing?</h2>
            <p>This cropped sketch came back in a real Tennessee records request. It shows the house, tank, and three field lines. Read it alongside the permit, any final approval, and later repair records.</p>
        </header>
        <div class="drawing-guide__layout">
            <figure class="drawing-guide__figure">
                <a href="/images/case-study/tn-historical-sketch-anonymized.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open the anonymized historical septic sketch at full size">
                    <img src="/images/case-study/tn-historical-sketch-anonymized.png" width="1110" height="560" loading="lazy" alt="Historical septic sketch with a house, tank beside the house, and three dashed field lines marked 60, 80, and 85 feet">
                </a>
                <figcaption>Original agency scan, with identifying header and road label cropped out. Open the image to enlarge it. The accompanying construction permit is not, by itself, proof of completed installation.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <dl class="drawing-guide__reading">
                <div><dt>House and tank</dt><dd>The sketch places a small tank beside the house. This is a historical location clue; confirm its position before excavation or construction.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Field lines</dt><dd>The three dashed runs are labeled 60, 80, and 85 feet. Together they total 225 feet of field line. That is a length, not a drain-field area.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Facts on a different page</dt><dd>The accompanying permit lists a 900-gallon tank and a three-bedroom design. Those details come from the permit text, not from measuring this sketch.</dd></div>
            </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="drawing-guide__types">
            <h3>A proposed plan and an as-built answer different questions</h3>
            <dl class="record-search-facts">
                <div><dt>Construction permit or approved plan</dt><dd>Describes authorized work. Check for installation, final approval, or operation records before treating it as the completed system.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>As-built or installation record</dt><dd>Documents the installed arrangement. Check its date, property match, and later alterations; it still does not establish today's condition.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Unlabeled site sketch</dt><dd>Ask the file owner whether it shows proposed or installed work. A drawing found in the packet is not automatically an as-built.</dd></div>
            </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="drawing-guide__next">
            <div>
                <h3>What if your file only contains the permit?</h3>
                <p>Ask for the installation drawing or as-built, final inspection or operation approval, and later repair records. Specify the missing document rather than requesting the same permit again.</p>
                <a href="/septic-permit-records-request/">See what to include in a records request</a>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>Want help connecting the pages?</h3>
                <p>Send the property and what you need to establish. We can investigate the file and explain what the documents confirm and what remains unanswered.</p>
                <a class="button button--primary" href="/offer-prep-septic-file-check/#record-help" data-record-help-cta data-track-click="nav" data-track-source-context="as_built_drawing_help" data-track-target-type="lead_form">Ask SepticPath to review the record</a>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="drawing-guide__provenance">Case source: the historical construction-permit packet and agency reply shown in our <a href="/#home-case-title">completed Tennessee request</a>. Personal identifiers are excluded. The example explains one returned file and does not establish another property's permit status.</p>
    </div>
</section>
